  Next real question — what "care about" means, because it's the one thing the request names and the one thing that
  decides the data model.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether caring is [3mderived[0m from your relationship to a task, or [3mdeclared[0m by an explicit action. Derived means
  no subscription is ever stored — you recompute "who cares" from the task itself. Declared means subscriptions are
  real records with their own lifecycle, and they're the thing that crosses the seam to a future server.

  - [1mDerived (creator/assignee)[0m — zero UI, nothing to store, nothing to sync. But in a single-user app you own
    everything, so you'd be notified about everything; it only starts discriminating once there are multiple people.
    Cheap to add later on top of declared; awkward as the only model right now.
  - [1mDeclared (watch/star a task)[0m — explicit, obvious, works identically in single- and multi-user worlds. Costs a stored
    [38;5;153msubscriptions[39m collection and a toggle in the UI. This is the one that makes the seam meaningful: a server
    implementation has something concrete to own.
  - [1mRule-based (saved filters: tag, due-soon, status)[0m — most expressive, and the only one that covers tasks that don't
    exist yet ("anything tagged urgent"). Costs a query/matcher layer and a rules UI. Hard to retrofit cleanly if we
    start elsewhere, but genuinely YAGNI-suspect for a first version.

  [1mMy recommendation: declared, with the matcher written so derived and rule-based are additive later.[0m Concretely, "who
  cares about this change" becomes one function over a list of subscription records; today every record is [38;5;153m{type: [39m
  [38;5;153m"task", id}[39m, and a future [38;5;153m{type: "rule", ...}[39m slots in without touching the event or delivery layers. That keeps the
  expensive-to-reverse part — the interface — open, without building the rules engine now.
